# Rift Challenge Backend Rules

## Stack
- Java 21
- Spring Boot
- Maven
- Spring Web
- Spring Data JPA
- PostgreSQL
- Flyway
- Bean Validation
- Spring Security when authentication is implemented
- Actuator for health checks

## Architecture
Prefer feature-oriented packages rather than one global package per technical layer.

Example:
- challenge/
- player/
- duo/
- account/
- authentication/
- riot/
- synchronization/
- common/

Within a feature, separate responsibilities clearly:
- controller
- service
- repository
- dto
- domain/entity where appropriate

Controllers should be thin.
Business rules belong in services/domain logic.
Repositories handle persistence only.

## API
- Use REST conventions.
- Use explicit request/response DTOs.
- Validate request DTOs.
- Return appropriate HTTP status codes.
- Do not expose JPA entities directly as public API contracts.
- Use a consistent error response format.
- Do not leak internal exceptions or secrets.

## Authentication
The application must support:
1. local account registration/login using email, username and password;
2. Google authentication.

Local passwords must be securely hashed.
Authentication/session implementation must be chosen deliberately; do not invent an insecure custom mechanism.

The user's email and username must have appropriate uniqueness constraints.
Never log passwords, tokens or OAuth secrets.

## Challenge rules
- SOLOQ challenge: maximum 16 players.
- DUOQ challenge: maximum 8 duos / 16 players.
- A DUOQ challenge represents pairs, not an unordered list of independent players.
- Challenge ownership must be enforced server-side.
- Only authorized users can modify their challenges.
- Challenge start date/time is persisted and compared server-side.
- Challenge status must never depend solely on frontend calculations.

## Refresh/rate limiting
The 2-minute challenge refresh rule must be enforced server-side.

A refresh request must:
- identify the challenge;
- verify that the requester is allowed to refresh it;
- check the last successful/accepted refresh time according to the chosen policy;
- reject or throttle refreshes made before the 2-minute window;
- return enough information for the frontend to display the next available refresh time.

Do not rely on the frontend button being disabled.

The implementation should also minimize Riot API calls by reusing persisted data.

## Riot integration
Never call Riot API directly from controllers when business logic is required.

Prefer:
Controller/Service
→ Riot client/service
→ Riot API

Handle:
- timeouts;
- HTTP errors;
- rate limits;
- missing data;
- transient failures.

Do not retry aggressively.

Riot API keys must come from environment/configuration and never from source code.

## Persistence
Use Flyway migrations.
Do not modify production schema manually.

Add database constraints for important invariants such as:
- unique email;
- unique username;
- challenge relationships;
- duo membership;
- appropriate foreign keys;
- valid ownership relationships.

Use indexes deliberately for frequent queries such as:
- challenge lookup;
- challenge participants;
- Riot account lookup;
- match lookup;
- refresh tracking.

## Testing
Business-critical services require tests.

Prioritize tests for:
- registration/authentication rules;
- challenge creation;
- 16-player SoloQ limit;
- 8-duo DuoQ limit;
- challenge ownership;
- challenge start state;
- refresh every 2 minutes;
- Riot match parsing;
- LP/rank calculations;
- DuoQ participant detection.

Mock Riot API calls in unit tests.
Use integration tests for database/API behavior where valuable.
